### 1. What You Can Deduct
*   **Home Office Deduction:** Because you have 1099-NEC freelance income, you can deduct your home office expenses against your consulting income (this goes on Schedule C, not your personal deductions). You have two choices:
    *   **Simplified Method:** The IRS allows $5 per square foot. For 165 sq ft, this gives you an **$825** deduction.
    *   **Actual Expenses Method:** This requires filling out **Form 8829**. Your office is roughly 23.9% of your total apartment (165 / 690 sq ft). Your total annual rent is $24,600 ($2,050 x 12). 23.9% of your rent equals **$5,882**. The actual expenses method gives you a much larger deduction, though it requires stricter record-keeping.
*   **Student Loan Interest:** The IRS caps the student loan interest deduction at **$2,500** (so you cannot deduct the full $2,700). Furthermore, this deduction phases out for single filers with a Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) between **$75,000 and $90,000**. Because your combined income (W-2 + 1099 + Interest) puts you right in the middle of this range, your $2,500 deduction will be partially reduced.
*   **Work Expenses ($650):** Unfortunately, W-2 employees cannot deduct unreimbursed work expenses. You cannot claim this $650.
*   **Charitable Donation ($720):** This is only deductible if you choose to itemize your deductions on Schedule A.

### 2. Should You Itemize or Take the Standard Deduction?
**You should take the Standard Deduction.** 
The standard deduction for a single filer in 2023 was $13,850, and it has increased to $14,600 for 2024. To make itemizing worth it, your individual deductions (like your $720 charity donation and state/local taxes) would need to exceed $14,600. 

*Good news:* You do not need to itemize to claim your Home Office Deduction or your Student Loan Interest Deduction. The home office is a business expense deducted from your 1099 income, and the student loan interest is an "above-the-line" adjustment to your income. You get to claim both of those *and* still take the full standard deduction!

